    "created_at": "2026-10-14T16:15:44Z",
    "updated_at": "2026-10-14T16:15:44Z",
    "body": "+1 to this proposal. Here's a concrete use case from business applications.\n\nMany users of legacy business / data-entry apps (e.g. very common in Japan) expect **Enter** to move to the next field, and entering data on a numeric keypad is much easier with Enter than Tab. I see this as an accessibility issue: the trigger key for sequential focus navigation is effectively fixed to Tab, so applications end up providing an \"Enter moves to the next field\" mode themselves.\n\nThe problem is that apps can't reliably know which element *would* receive focus next. Userland approximations (e.g. `tabbable`) are inevitably incomplete — for example, whether keyboard-focusable scrollers participate in sequential navigation differs between browsers, and this can't be feature-detected. So Enter and Tab can end up with different orders. `nextFocusable()` / `previousFocusable()` would let apps offer an alternative key that behaves exactly like Tab.\n\nOne question: since this kind of navigation is usually scoped to a form or section, would container-scoped traversal (e.g. `nextFocusable({ within: container })` returning `null` at the boundary) be in scope?",
